('Two policies generate the same total surplus, but one gives nearly all gains to high-income buyers while the other spreads gains broadly. Surplus analysis alone can conclude that:', ['They are equally efficient by total surplus, but it cannot settle the equity comparison','The first policy is necessarily better because buyers have more income','The second policy is necessarily more efficient because gains are spread broadly','Neither policy can be compared in any way'], 'They are equally efficient by total surplus, but it cannot settle the equity comparison','Equal total surplus means equal measured efficiency, while distribution requires an additional normative criterion.','efficiency_equity_distinction'),
